\title{Set types in Disco}
\date{2026-02-19T22:53:33Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{The [type](002B) of a finite set with elements of type \code{T} is written \code{Set(T)}. For example:}

\pre\verb<<<|
Disco> :type {1,2,3}
{1, 2, 3} : Set(ℕ)
Disco> :type {T, F}
{T, F} : Set(Bool)<<<

\p{All the elements in a set have to have the same type.  For example, this is an error:}

\pre\verb<<<|
Disco> {1, T}
Error: typechecking failed.<<<

\p{However, we can mix and match different numeric types, and Disco will just give them the smallest type that works for all of them.}

\pre\verb<<<|
Disco> :type {1,2}
{1, 2} : Set(ℕ)
Disco> :type {3, 4, -5}
{3, 4, -5} : Set(ℤ)
Disco> :type {2, 1/2}
{2, 1 / 2} : Set(𝔽)
Disco> :type {2, -1, 3, 5/2}
{2, -1, 3, 5 / 2} : Set(ℚ)<<<

\p{If we ask for the type of the empty set, Disco responds with \code{Set(a)}: the \code{a} here is a \em{variable} that can stand for \em{any} type.  This is our first example of \em{polymorphism}.}

\pre\verb<<<|
Disco> :type {}
{} : Set(a)<<<
